//
// What `loft --tests` actually runs (loft#1010).
//
// The catalogue says "any function named `test_*` is a test"; TESTING.md says the harness
// discovers every zero-parameter function and calls only `main` when one exists.  Measured,
// the shipped runner matches neither: it runs EVERY function of arity zero, `main` included,
// counts each in the total, and a failing `assert` in any of them fails the file.  A function
// that takes an argument is the only thing it skips.
//
// This file is itself the instrument.  It has no `fn main`, so every function below is an
// entry point — and `not_a_test_by_arity` proves the rule by being the one that is NOT run:
// it takes a parameter, and its body asserts something false.  If the runner's rule ever
// becomes name-based, this file keeps passing; if arity stops being the filter, the parameter
// cell fires.  The counterpart — a zero-argument non-`test_` function that IS run — is
// `records_that_it_ran`, whose own assertion only holds when it has been called.
//
// The counted total is the other half and cannot be asserted from inside: the runner prints
// `(3 fns: …)` for the three zero-parameter functions here, and a name-based rule would print
// one.  That number is the cheapest check of the rule and it lives in the run's output.

// @FTR-056 — every zero-parameter function is an entry point, and a parameter is what opts out
fn test_the_ordinary_shape() {
  assert(1 + 1 == 2, "a test named test_* runs, which is the undisputed half");
}

// No `test_` prefix, no parameters — run all the same.  A helper that asserts a precondition
// is an ordinary thing to write and it becomes a test here.
fn records_that_it_ran() {
  // Nothing to compare against a previous run — each entry point gets a fresh program — so
  // this asserts only that it can execute, which is what being CALLED means.
  n = 0;
  for i in 1..4 { n += i; }
  assert(n == 6, "a zero-parameter function with no test_ prefix runs too, got {n}");
}

// Takes a parameter, so it is NOT an entry point.  The assertion is deliberately false: if
// arity ever stops being the filter, this file goes red and says so by name.
fn not_a_test_by_arity(unused: integer) {
  assert(unused == unused + 1, "loft#1010: a function WITH a parameter must not be run");
}

// Returns a value and takes none — still an entry point, so the value is discarded and only
// its assertions matter.
fn returns_something() -> integer {
  v = 21 * 2;
  assert(v == 42, "a value-returning zero-parameter function runs, got {v}");
  v
}
